            <ArticleTitle>Clinical implications of pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of procedural sedation agents in children.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>225-32</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/MOP.0b013e3283504f88</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE OF REVIEW" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Procedural sedation has become the standard of care for managing pain and anxiety in children in the emergency department.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RECENT FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Numerous articles have been published on pediatric procedural sedation with, however, little in-depth discussion of the pharmacodynamics and pharmacokinetics of the sedation agents utilized.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We review the pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of the pediatric procedural sedation pharmacopeia from a clinical perspective with emphasis on the practical implications for drug titration and dosing.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
